  "title": "<p class=\"ql-align-center\"><strong style=\"color: rgb(38, 38, 38);\">“﻿Feeling Bad”&nbsp;About What You’ve Done: </strong><span style=\"color: rgb(38, 38, 38);\">Investigating the Relationship Between Moral Threat, Social Withdrawal, and Repair in the Aftermath of Wrongdoing</span></p><p><br></p>",
  "abstract": "<p>When a person fails to do right by another, they often experience it as a moral threat — an experience which calls into question their sense of themselves a good person who does good things. Shame and guilt are important ways in which people emotionally experience these threats. For centuries, both emotions have been used across cultures to punish problematic social behavior, often in hopes of eliciting better behavior in the future. It’s not uncommon, for example, to hear a parent admonish a misbehaving child, “You ought to be ashamed of yourself” or, “Go and think about what you’ve done.” As a social species reliant on shared norms and cooperation, it is of course in all our interest that people who have harmed others make amends for their actions. Nonetheless, the roles shame and guilt play in actually promoting such action after an incidence of interpersonal wrongdoing remain unclear. This may result from the fact that the two emotions are often spoken of in the same breath, and in fact are quite often experienced together in the aftermath of moral wrongdoing. However, important new cleavages allow us to see meaningful distinctions between shame and guilt and elicit important questions about the emotions’ differential influences on interpersonal conflict. </p>",
